# Agent Workspace — deployment values.
#
#   cp .env.example .env      # then fill the deployment-specific blanks below
#   docker compose up -d
#
# ── What belongs in this file, and what does not ────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Spec §37 is the whole point of this file:
#
#   > Avoid making users edit `.env` for normal application configuration.
#   > Environment variables should primarily be for deployment-level values.
#   > Agent-specific configuration belongs in the database.
#
# So: which model an agent uses, which tools it may call, which skills and MCP connectors it
# loads, which runtime profile it runs on, who may see it — none of that is here, and none of it
# ever should be. It is edited in the UI and stored in Postgres, and it is compiled into Agent SDK
# options by `packages/core/src/config.ts`. If you are reaching for this file to change how an
# agent behaves, the answer is a screen, not a variable.
#
# This file holds deployment values only. §37's illustrative list names four of them. The
# remaining values secure bootstrap and object storage;
# without them the deployment starts
# and refuses every API request, which is correct behaviour for an auth gate and a useless first
# run. The reasoning, the rejected alternatives and the honest downside are in
# docs/adr/W1-INF-01-env-example-is-six-values.md.

# ── The four §37 names ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Read by `packages/core/src/db/client.ts`.
# The host `postgres` is the compose service; it resolves inside the compose network and nowhere
# else. Running a migration from your own shell instead? Point this at a published Postgres — this
# deployment does not publish one, on purpose.
DATABASE_URL=postgres://agent_workspace:agent_workspace@postgres:5432/agent_workspace

# Spec §23's key for encrypting stored secrets at rest.
#   Generate with:  openssl rand -hex 32
#   Leave it empty or set it correctly: a set but malformed value fails GET /api/readyz.
# Set MASTER_KEY_PROVIDER=kms on AWS, secret-manager on GCP, or key-vault on Azure to load the
# same-shaped key through workload identity; then leave MASTER_ENCRYPTION_KEY empty and configure
# that provider.
MASTER_KEY_PROVIDER=local-key
GCP_MASTER_KEY_SECRET=
MASTER_ENCRYPTION_KEY=
AZURE_KEY_VAULT_URL=
AZURE_MASTER_KEY_SECRET_NAME=
# For kms only. The ciphertext is base64 from `aws kms encrypt`; never put the plaintext here.
AWS_KMS_KEY_ID=
AWS_KMS_ENCRYPTED_MASTER_KEY=

# Where files, skills and session state are kept: one URL (the storage design, §04). Credentials
# never go in it; they stay in AWS_* or the cloud's workload identity. Examples:
#   AW_STORAGE_URL=file:///var/lib/agent-workspace
#   AW_STORAGE_URL=gcs://agent-workspace?project=my-project
#   AW_STORAGE_URL=azblob://myaccount/agent-workspace
# The value below is compose.yaml's bundled `seaweedfs`: the bucket, endpoint and path style the old
# settings gave it, so existing objects stay where they are. Its publicEndpoint is the host port
# compose.yaml publishes SeaweedFS on.
AW_STORAGE_URL=s3://agent-workspace?endpoint=http://seaweedfs:8333&publicEndpoint=http://localhost:8333&pathStyle=true
# AW_STORAGE_CREATE_BUCKET=true makes the server create the bucket of an s3:// AW_STORAGE_URL on
# first boot, idempotently; compose.yaml sets it for the bundled `seaweedfs`. Unset or false never
# creates one; any other value stops start-up. It is a server setting, never part of the URL.
# The old storage location variables (docs/sdk/site/reference/contracts/storage-settings.mdx lists
# them) are ignored: each one still set logs one storage_setting_ignored warning that names it,
# never its value, and `agent-workspace doctor` prints the AW_STORAGE_URL line that replaces them.
# With AW_STORAGE_URL unset nothing is read from the old location: the files, skills and
# session-state APIs are not installed and answer 503.
# Files an older install wrote have no catalog row, and lists read the catalog. So at start-up the
# server gives each one a row in the background (the storage design, §06): unset (or adopt) runs
# it. It writes nothing to the bucket, never brings back a removed file, and is safe to repeat.
# Until the run finishes after an upgrade, such a file may be missing from lists. Set off to
# disable it.
#   AW_STORAGE_MIGRATE=off
#
# Storage cleanup runs one server at a time: hourly on a timer, or from the platform cron calling
# GET /api/cron/storage-cleanup where CRON_SECRET is set (daily in the generated Vercel config). Unset or report only
# counts the objects no file row points at; delete removes them. Files in the old naming are
# counted and never deleted. Read one report before setting delete.
#   AW_STORAGE_CLEANUP=delete
#
# The key pair is also the `seaweedfs` service's only S3 identity (compose.yaml reads these two for
# both ends), so an unsigned request to it is refused. Replace the secret before exposure:
#   Generate with:  openssl rand -base64 24
OBJECT_STORAGE_ACCESS_KEY_ID=agent_workspace
OBJECT_STORAGE_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=agent_workspace

# The first workspace owner. `packages/core/src/db/seed.ts` inserts this person as the `owner` of
# the seeded workspace on first start; `apps/web/server/auth/session.ts` accepts it as the sign-in
# identity. Change it before you start, or you will be signing in as someone at `example.com`.
BOOTSTRAP_ADMIN=admin@example.com

# ── The two the bootstrap gate requires ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# HMAC key for the session cookie. At least 32 characters, or every /api/** request is refused
# with 401 and the server log names this variable. There is no generated fallback: a key invented
# at start-up silently invalidates every session on restart and cannot work across replicas.
# Rotating this value is also the ONLY way to revoke sessions already issued.
#   Generate with:  openssl rand -hex 32
SESSION_SECRET=

# Optional. Scopes the sign-in cookie to this deployment: `__Host-aw_session_<id>` instead of
# `__Host-aw_session`. Browsers share cookies across ports, so two deployments on one host (two
# projects on localhost:3000 and localhost:3002) sign each other out unless each sets its own.
# Lowercase letters, digits, "-" and "_". Changing it signs everyone out once.
# AW_INSTANCE_ID=

# Exact count of trusted reverse proxies that append X-Forwarded-For before a request reaches the
# web process. Runnable public shares fail closed when this is absent or invalid; never guess this
# value or enable it on an origin that clients can reach directly.
AW_TRUSTED_PROXY_HOPS=

# Optional. The https origin external agent platforms reach this deployment at, with no path
# (`https://aw.example.com`; `http://localhost:3000` is accepted for local work). The A2A agent card
# at /api/a2a/agents/<name>/.well-known/agent-card.json builds its `url` from it; unset, that route
# answers 503 and A2A JSON-RPC calls still work. It is also the OAuth 2.1 issuer for endpoint clients
# (ADR-0134): unset, /oauth/* and the /.well-known/oauth-* documents answer 404 oauth_not_configured.
# AW_PUBLIC_URL=

RUNNER_MANAGER_TOKEN=

# Immutable price catalogue used to settle recorded provider usage. The sentinel lets the gateway
# become healthy but matches no real provider/model: runs still run, and each one settles unpriced
# (cost 0.000000, no priceTableVersion, accountingState "unavailable") until an operator replaces it
# with reviewed provider:model rates, and a new version whenever prices change.
GATEWAY_PRICING_JSON={"version":"unconfigured","models":{"disabled:unconfigured":{"inputUsdPerMillion":"0","outputUsdPerMillion":"0"}}}

# The bootstrap administrator's password. At least 12 characters. Never written to the database —
# it is compared, in constant time, against what the sign-in form was given. One shared credential
# is inherent to a bootstrap administrator; §21 makes OIDC the real answer.
#   Generate with:  openssl rand -base64 24
BOOTSTRAP_ADMIN_PASSWORD=

# ── Not here, and deliberately ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# There is no ANTHROPIC_API_KEY in this file. Spec A.11 is explicit that a provider master
# credential never enters a sandbox: the model gateway holds it and injects a short-lived,
# run-scoped `ANTHROPIC_AUTH_TOKEN` instead. Provider credentials are entered through setup or the
# Models screen, encrypted at rest, and resolved only by the model gateway.
